The {{nihongo|'''Okaiken Basketball Super League'''|岡井県バスケットボール・スーパーリーグ|Okain Basukettbōru Sūpā Rīgu}}, officially abbreviated as '''OBSL''', is a men's professional basketball league in [[Okaiken]]. The league is operated by the [[Okaiken Professional Basketball League]] and was formed as a result of a merger between the [[National Basketball League (Okaiken)|National Basketball League]] that was operated by the [[Okaiken Basketball Association]] and the independently operated [[Industrial Basketball Federation]]. The league began play in September 2016.

The OBSL is the premier professional basketball league in Okaiken, consisting of three divisions: OBSL1 (first division), OBSL2 (second division), and OBSL3 (developmental third division). As of the 2025–26 season, the league comprises 53 teams across all divisions. The OBSL has a system of promotion and relegation between OBSL1 and OBSL2.

==History==

===Origins and corporate basketball era (1967–2005)===

The [[Okaiken Basketball Association]] was formed in 1930 and operated Okaiken's top basketball leagues under various names since 1967. Throughout the history of the association, teams were affiliated with large corporations and industrial conglomerates, with players employed by their respective owner companies rather than competing as professional basketball players. This corporate structure mirrored similar arrangements in Sanese baseball and was rooted in the post-war industrialization period.

The [[National Basketball League (Okaiken)|National Basketball League]] (NBL) was established in 1967 as the top-flight basketball competition, initially featuring eight corporate teams including the [[Toyozawa Steel Dolphins]] (now Toyozawa Iron Rams), [[Mito Machinery Kings]] (now Mito Royals), and [[Okami Electric Sentinels]] (now Okami Sentinels). The league operated with a semi-professional structure, with players technically employed as company workers who played basketball as part of their duties.

In the early 1990s, following the successful professionalization of football in Okaiken with the [[Sanese Soccer League]] launch in 1993, the Okaiken Basketball Association commenced investigating the professionalization of basketball. In 1997, the association lifted the ban on professional players, though the NBL structure remained largely corporate in nature.

===Formation of the Industrial Basketball Federation (1989–2015)===

In 1989, a group of industrial companies from the [[Toyozawa]] and [[Mito]] regions formed the [[Industrial Basketball Federation]] (IBF) as an alternative competition structure. Unlike the corporate-focused NBL, the IBF operated on a franchise model similar to professional leagues the United Midlands, with teams representing cities rather than companies. The IBF initially featured 12 teams and emphasized a more entertainment-oriented product, with imported players and aggressive marketing.

The IBF's popularity grew throughout the 1990s and 2000s, particularly in industrial cities where basketball was embraced as part of the local working-class culture. Teams such as the [[Toyozawa Iron Rams]] (originally Toyozawa Forge), [[Kurosaki Steelworkers]], and [[Ehime Dockers]] developed strong regional followings independent of corporate sponsorship.

By 2005, the IBF had expanded to 18 teams and was attracting average attendance figures comparable to the NBL, despite operating with significantly smaller corporate backing. This created tensions within Sanese basketball, as the two leagues competed for players, venues, and television coverage.

===AIBA intervention and league merger (2014–2016)===

[[AIBA]], the international governing body for basketball, grew concerned with the division and disorganization of the sport within Okaiken. After the Okaiken Basketball Association failed to comply with deadlines to commence reorganizing the domestic leagues, AIBA suspended Okaiken from international competitions in November 2014. The suspension meant Okaiken could not participate in the [[AIBA Fosia Championship]] or qualify for the [[Basketball at the Summer Olympics|Olympic basketball tournament]].

A task force to investigate the reformation of the domestic leagues was formed, with [[Saburō Kawabuchi]] appointed as co-chairman alongside former OBSL player [[Kenji Watanabe]]. In May 2015, upon AIBA's recommendation, [[Shinji Shimada]] was appointed as president of the Okaiken Basketball Association.

The merger of the two competing leagues into the Okaiken Basketball Super League was announced in June 2015, and the international suspension was lifted by AIBA in August 2015. Telecommunications company [[Sanese Telecom]] was named as the league's top sponsor for the inaugural season in March 2016.

===Inaugural season and establishment (2016–2020)===

The 2016–17 OBSL season commenced with an inaugural match between five-time NBL champions [[Tsuda Lakers]], who finished on top of the NBL ladder in 2015–16, and four-time IBF champions [[Toyozawa Iron Rams]], who won the 2015–16 IBF championship, at the [[Okami National Gymnasium]] on September 22, 2016. The Iron Rams won the match 89–84 before a crowd of 18,500 spectators. A full round of games involving all other teams commenced on September 24.

The inaugural season featured 46 teams across three divisions, with the first division (OBSL1) containing 24 teams divided into two conferences. The [[Tsuda Lakers]] won the first OBSL championship, defeating the [[Okami Sentinels]] in the finals series 3–1.

The early years of the OBSL saw significant growth in attendance and media coverage, with average attendance for OBSL1 games increasing from 3,200 in the inaugural season to 4,800 by the 2019–20 season. The league also established partnerships with corporate sponsors aligned with the [[National Iron Body Initiative]], positioning basketball as a vehicle for physical fitness and national athletic development.

===Expansion and reorganization (2020–present)===

The 2020–21 season was affected by the global pandemic, with games played in controlled environments without spectators for portions of the season. Despite these challenges, the league maintained its schedule and introduced several innovations, including enhanced digital streaming capabilities and the "Iron Guard" health protocols developed in conjunction with the [[Ministry of Health and Welfare (Okaiken)|Ministry of Health and Welfare]].

Beginning in the 2021–22 season, the winners and runners-up of OBSL1 qualified for the [[East Fosia Super League]], providing Sanese teams with opportunities to compete against top professional clubs from neighboring countries.

The 2022–23 season saw the introduction of a restructured conference system in OBSL1, with three conferences (East, Central, and West) replacing the previous two-conference format. This reorganization was designed to reduce travel costs and enhance regional rivalries.

==Season format==

The league consists of three divisions: OBSL1, OBSL2, and OBSL3. OBSL1 and OBSL2 feature professional players, while OBSL3 serves as a developmental league with semi-professional players and younger prospects.

===OBSL1 (First Division)===

OBSL1 comprises 24 teams divided into three conferences: East, Central, and West. Each team plays a 60-game regular-season schedule consisting of:

* 36 games against teams within their conference (4 games against each of the 8 other teams)
* 24 games against teams from other conferences (2 games against 10 teams, 4 games against 2 teams)

Eight teams qualify for the playoffs:
* The top two teams from each conference (6 teams total)
* The next two teams with the best records regardless of conference (2 wild card teams)

The playoffs consist of quarterfinal, semifinal, and final rounds, with a best-of-three format in each round. Each round is played at the home court of the team that finished with the higher winning percentage during the regular season. The finals series is a best-of-five format.

===OBSL2 (Second Division)===

OBSL2 comprises 14 teams divided into two conferences: East and West. Each team plays a 60-game regular-season schedule with a similar format to OBSL1. Eight teams qualify for playoffs under identical rules to the first division, with the winner earning automatic promotion to OBSL1.

===OBSL3 (Developmental Division)===

OBSL3 comprises 15 teams operating as developmental affiliates of OBSL1 and OBS2 clubs. Players in OBSL3 are typically under contract with parent clubs and may be called up during the season. OBSL3 operates a 48-game schedule with playoffs for the top four teams.

===Promotion and relegation===

The OBSL maintains a promotion and relegation system between OBSL1 and OBSL2:

* The OBSL2 champion is automatically promoted to OBSL1
* The OBSL2 runner-up enters a promotion playoff against the 23rd-place team from OBSL1
* The 24th-place team from OBSL1 is automatically relegated to OBSL2

This system ensures competitive balance and provides pathways for smaller-market teams to reach the top division.

