==Rules and regulations==

===Foreign players===

Each club in OBSL1 and OBSL2 is allowed up to three registered foreign players, excluding one foreign-born player who has become a naturalized Sanese citizen. Two foreign players are allowed on the court simultaneously. Naturalized players can play as Sanese citizens and have no limitations. Each club is allowed one naturalized player.

In line with Okaiken Basketball Association regulations, foreign citizens who were either born or raised in Okaiken and graduated from Sanese elementary and junior high school are not treated as foreign players for the purpose of these rules.

===Salary cap and contracts===

OBSL1 operates with a soft salary cap of ¥450 million per team per season. The cap was introduced in the 2018–19 season to promote competitive balance and prevent wealthier franchises from dominating player acquisition. Teams may exceed the cap by up to 15% to retain their own free agents under "Iron Rights" provisions.

Player contracts are guaranteed, with a maximum length of five years for players over 25 and three years for players under 25. The minimum salary for OBSL1 players is ¥4.8 million per season, while OBSL2 players have a minimum of ¥2.4 million.

===Player draft===

The annual [[OBSL Draft]] is held each June, featuring eligible players from university programs, high schools, and international prospects. The draft consists of two rounds, with draft order determined by inverse order of regular-season finish for non-playoff teams, followed by elimination order for playoff teams.

Notable first overall picks include:
* 2016: [[Ryosuke Kamiya]] (Tsuda Lakers) – 3× OBSL MVP
* 2018: [[Hayato Endo]] (Toyozawa Iron Rams) – 2024 Most Improved Player
* 2020: [[Kenta Matsui]] (Mito Royals) – 2023 Finals MVP

==Awards and honors==

===Individual awards===

The OBSL presents the following individual awards at the conclusion of each regular season:

* '''[[OBSL Most Valuable Player Award]]''' – Most valuable player
* '''[[OBSL Defensive Player of the Year Award]]''' – Best defensive player (won 3× by [[Taiga Himura]])
* '''[[OBSL Rookie of the Year Award]]''' – Best first-year player
* '''[[OBSL Most Improved Player Award]]''' – Player showing most improvement
* '''[[OBSL Sixth Man Award]]''' – Best player coming off the bench
* '''[[OBSL Coach of the Year Award]]''' – Best head coach

===All-Star Game===

The [[OBSL All-Star Game]] is held annually in February, featuring the top players from each conference. The event includes the All-Star Game itself, a three-point contest, slam dunk contest, and skills challenge. The 2025 All-Star Game was held at [[Toyozawa Metropolitan Arena]] and drew a record attendance of 22,500 spectators.

==Venues and attendance==

The OBSL requires OBSL1 teams to play in arenas with a minimum capacity of 5,000 spectators. Several franchises have constructed new venues since the league's founding:

* '''[[Tsuda Metropolitan Arena]]''' (Tsuda Lakers) – 18,500 capacity, opened 2018
* '''[[Toyozawa Forge Arena]]''' (Toyozawa Iron Rams) – 15,000 capacity, opened 2019
* '''[[Okami National Gymnasium]]''' (Okami Sentinels) – 16,000 capacity, renovated 2017
* '''[[Mito Steel Arena]]''' (Mito Royals) – 12,000 capacity, opened 2020
* '''[[Kochi Imperial Arena]]''' (Kochi Imperials) – 11,500 capacity, opened 2021

