==International competition==

===East Fosia Super League===

Since the 2021–22 season, OBSL1 champions and runners-up qualify for the [[East Fosia Super League]] (EASL), competing against top professional clubs from neighboring countries. OBSL teams have achieved the following results in EASL competition:

* '''2021–22''': Tsuda Lakers – Semifinals
* '''2022–23''': Kochi Imperials – Champions; Tsuda Lakers – Quarterfinals
* '''2023–24''': Mito Royals – Runners-up; Tsuda Lakers – Semifinals
* '''2024–25''': Mito Royals – Semifinals; Toyozawa Iron Rams – Quarterfinals

===National team===

OBSL players form the core of the [[Okaiken men's national basketball team]], which competes in AIBA Fosia competitions. The national team's best recent result was a bronze medal at the [[2023 AIBA Fosia Championship]]. The team is coached by former Tsuda Lakers head coach [[Masahiro Tachibana]].

==Media coverage==

===Television===

The OBSL's primary broadcast partners are [[OKB]] (Okaiken Broadcasting) and the [[KTK]] (KTK). Games are broadcast nationally on free-to-air and cable platforms, with OKB holding rights to the All-Star Game, playoffs, and finals.

In 2023, the OBSL signed a five-year broadcasting agreement worth ¥12 billion with OKB, representing the largest media rights deal in Sanese basketball history.

===Digital streaming===

The league operates [[OBSL Stream]], a subscription-based streaming platform offering live and on-demand coverage of all OBSL1 and OBSL2 games. The platform launched in 2020 and had over 450,000 subscribers as of 2025.

==Corporate sponsorship==

The OBSL maintains partnerships with several major Sanese corporations:

* [[Sanese Telecom]] – League title sponsor (2016–present)
* [[Toyozawa Heavy Industries]] – Official equipment partner
* [[Mito Steel]] – Official steel supplier for arena construction
* [[Okami Electric]] – Official technology partner
* [[Ehime Shipping]] – Official logistics partner

Total sponsorship revenue for the 2024–25 season was estimated at ¥8.5 billion, representing a significant increase from the ¥3.2 billion in the inaugural season.

